{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/622428-where-i-read-hunters-of-the-edo-jidai","id":622428,"title":"Where I Read: Hunters of the Edo Jidai","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Hunter: The Vigil"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-08-10T11:02:12-07:00","id":622428,"title":"Where I Read: Hunters of the Edo Jidai","content":"<span style=\"font-size:20px\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">In this thread I will read through the Hunters of the Edo Jidai which can be found in <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/622019-six-compacts-and-conspiracies-from-the-edo-jidai\">this preview</a> </span><br /> <br /> Ama San</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Opening</span><br /> <br /> In the start we are quickly introduced to perl divers and the sea whistle. There is nothing specifically Hunter in this opening, which makes sense.<br /> <br /> The average WoD reader can be assumed to know what a Trade Union is but not about Japanease perl diving (I did, but not about the sea whistle). This opening quickly introduces the real world concepts behind the Hunter Compact.<br /> <br /> Also. It's about damn time we got something on underwater monsters <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> so I'm excited to read on.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">The Enemy</span><br /> <br /> I've found that &quot;The Enemy&quot; sections usually go one of two ways. Either they talk about the hunter's tactics, or about who the hunter's target.<br /> <br /> This section is mostly a who they target, but with a bit of tactics there too. Suprisingly we're told that they they have a revernce of sorts for the sea's eccological balance (I presume eccological balance is not unique to the sea, rather it's a unique trait of the Ama San too care about it).<br /> <br /> So who do they target? Sea monsters who grow too big and disrupt this ecological balance. I say suprising because they give me a bit of a Unionish feel (Hunters organised around a working class profession) and it's not like the Union to care about why a monster is killing thier own.<br /> <br /> Next we get to tactics, which are mostly &quot;lure them to the surface, then kill them&quot;. Don't let the fact these monsters are described as causing tusnami's and earthquakes break your disbelief. Look up Isonade on Wikipedia, quite a small thing for having so much power.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Hunters</span><br /> <br /> We have three hunters here:<br /> <br /> A sixty year old aproaching retirement but hoping to die in the hunt or be dragged of by a monster lover.<br /> A young girl who's got a psychic link to a monster, that the compact is promising to kill and free her from.<br /> A token boy.<br /> <br /> While I've often thought the &quot;Hunters&quot; section is not the best use of precious precious wordcount, these three show the section at it's best - giving not only character ideas but non-obvious ideas and some much needed hints of what sea monsters they could expect to find (the obligatory lovecraftian monster makes its appearance \u2013 are ningyo that lovecraftian in folklore?). The token boy in particular manages to provide useful information on the Compact as a whole (how they treat male members) without lessening the character\u2019s usefulness as a character. I preferred this method to the sidebar used by the Maiden's Blood Sisterhood.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Subgroups</span><br /> <br /> As always we have three:<br /> <br /> Deep Divers - scouts and bait<br /> Callers - use the sea whistle to make monsters surface (after they've lured into hearing range by Deep Divers)<br /> Okaa-San - Perform the actual killing, and also sell the monsters they catch.<br /> <br /> There's a lot more about their tactics embedded into these groups. Most importantly, the sea whistle lures monsters ashore before they kill them. Actually, that's a little disapointing, I was hoping for awesome underwater fights (also, how many sea monsters are amphibious? It can't be many).<br /> <br /> So what I like about these three is they make it feel like an organisation. I'd estimate with this division of duties you'd need at least 9 people per hunt which plays nicely to the idea that Hunter's greatest advnatage is numbers.<br /> <br /> What I don't like is that these groups feel far too narrow. If you take it literally a Caller's only job during the hunt is wait, make one roll, then get out of there. I don't see any reason why a single hunter couldn't do all three roles in a single hunt. It would probably be more interesting for players and free up the subgroups to explore different directions you could take the core compact like they usually are. For example there's a bit about the Okaa-San selling their kills to occultists - that would make a great subgroup all by itself; a country bumpkin (I think Ami-San are that) going into town to sell their catch to sinister sorcerers or hire out the compact as mercenaries to sceptical samurai is practically an entire separate game to hunting sea monsters but it would be awesome. That kind of story defining choice is what I like to see when choosing my subgroups.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Status</span><br /> <br /> Status O: Small Unit Tactics at an enhanced level.<br /> Status OOO: You can use the sea whistle to force monsters to surface<br /> Status OOOOO: retainers show up when you're in danger<br /> <br /> Now at this point we start to see the compact suffering from word count. The first dot of Status gives Small Unit Tactics. Now this is an interesting choice because I always imagined Hunters would be getting that Merit for free; the core powerset of the Hunter line is called Tactics after all. Anyway, I said &quot;wordcount&quot; - well if every hunter has Tactics, what makes the Ami-Sama better than other hunters? What do they have that the military training of VALKARIE does not provide?<br /> <br /> Well a perfectly sensible answer is provided. They grew up together and learned to be a team from childhood; but that answer only has one sentence dedicated to it. The token guy's section in Hunters also touches upon it \u2013 but only barely through inference.<br /> <br /> <br /> Status three I don't like too much. There's no link or justification given to link Status to the sea whistle. The Sea Whistle would be much better as an Endowment or a Tactic (perhaps an Endowment that usees the Tactic mechanics)<br /> <br /> Status five gives you six dots worth of Retainers; who apparently have the ability to teleport (they turn up within nine seconds if you're in danger). The retainers make sense given the close knit nature of the compact but the teleportation doesn't. I'd strongly suggest dropping that bit and just giving them Retainers; players can easily ask their retainers to stay close.<br /> <br /> I also notice that compared to to other hunters the Ama San get a LOT of benefits from their Status. Small Unit Tactics is 3 dots, but two dots is unusually high for Hunters' one dot benefit. Getting a retainer at 5 dots has been done before, but never so many dots of it.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Closing thoughts</span><br /> <br /> This Compact is bursting with ideas, most of them good, to the point that there isn't enough room to give all these ideas room to breath.<br /> <br /> Case in point. The idea of a family oriented Compact has been done before. But Ama San do it better than Heratage House \u2013 despite having only one sentence about family. And I'm saying that seriously. Having more than just \u201cfamily\u201d helps a lot, and the things that aren't explicit but easily extrapolated \u2013 like growing up together creating advantages in Tactics &amp; Small Unit Tactics provide a superior take on family business to Heritage House's cultish tendencies. (The Scarlet Watch don't count. They not family, they're descendants).<br /> <br /> So imagine what we could get if there was room to devote three or so paragraphs to growing up Ama San.<br /> <br /> I'd say the only things that really suffered were the subgroups being awkwardly welded to tactics since using the Subgroups this way means they can't be used to provide more diversity and splitting their hunt into three separate groups resulted in them having exactly one tactic for a hunt; what happens if a monster is immune to the sea whistle or can't get close to the shore (e.g. a Ban)?<br /> <br /> The sea whistle being awkwardly made into a Status Benefit is, well it's merely awkward and with more wordcount it could easily be moved elsewhere or expanded with fluff to connect it to Status. I imagine the bit about the balance of the sea will mostly be ignored since there's no material on it. With an explanation of what it is and why the Ami San care it could have been good, but it's absence does no harm. Same for the family aspects, groups that notice it should be able to fill in the gaps and groups that don't won't miss too much.<br /> <br /> <br /> One final thought is that \u2013 obviously \u2013 these women (and token guy) are going to become more useful and more playable the more other books expand upon cool aquatic monsters to fight.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">The inevitable section on Leviathan</span><br /> <br /> So how would the Ami San tie in to Leviathan? Thematically they'd actually do great. Practically, a Transformed Leviathan with 2E mechanics (once they're written) has nothing to fear from them.<br /> <br /> Thematically they're draw power (namely Small Unit Tactics) from being an insular and close knit community. That makes a nice foil to Leviathans who's insular and small knit communities are unhealthy cults.<br /> <br /> But as I said. Even a starting Leviathan (if they chose combat powers) would probably wipe the floor with dozens of Ami San and depending on how the sea whistle works \u2013 Leviathans are going to be all but immune to mental influence.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Other aquatic beasties</span><br /> <br /> There's a fair few monsters that are aquatic; how do the Ami San thematically tie to them? Mostly I'd say they don't. When I think what's the core thematics of the Ami San; it's that they hunt underwater and \u2013 despite it being barely mentioned; their family and tight knit community.<br /> <br /> The only other underwater splat presented where family really matters are Changelings \u2013 but Ami San's take on family is unrelated to abduction or the quest for a new life.<br /> <br /> (Someone's going to mention Beast. I pre-emtively disagree that Beast actually has family themes; and will leave it at that).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cThere are no rules. Only Principles and natural laws.\u201d - Promethius<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/17686-the-kings-raven-s-homebrwe-index\" target=\"_blank\">My Homebrew no longer fits in a signature, you can find an index of it here</a>.<br /> <b>Full length fan-books I contributed too: </b><a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/princessthehopeful/files/PrincessTheHopeful.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Princess: the Hopeful</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/0B9DMG5y7ZdngekVaMGNQbTF4RzA\" target=\"_blank\">Leviathan: the Tempest</a>, <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/thekingsraven/files/DreamCatchers.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Dream Catchers</a>","edited":{"userid":134,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/134-the-kings-raven","user":"The Kings Raven","