le":"","content":"Thanks for covering for me Arc. I'll edit that link into the top. And I'll see you all soon with tomorrow's entry <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cThere are no rules. Only Principles and natural laws.\u201d - Promethius<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/17686-the-kings-raven-s-homebrwe-index\" target=\"_blank\">My Homebrew no longer fits in a signature, you can find an index of it here</a>.<br /> <b>Full length fan-books I contributed too: </b><a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/princessthehopeful/files/PrincessTheHopeful.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Princess: the Hopeful</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/0B9DMG5y7ZdngekVaMGNQbTF4RzA\" target=\"_blank\">Leviathan: the Tempest</a>, <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/thekingsraven/files/DreamCatchers.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Dream Catchers</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/134-the-kings-raven","name":"The Kings Raven","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar134_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3003"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-10T14:55:54-07:00","id":622692,"title":"","content":"You might want to condense and repost these critiques in the thread David made, because some of the stuff, like the bits on their subgroups or the structuring of their status benefits might be useful feedback.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-10T18:04:03-07:00","id":622944,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Leetsepeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/622428-where-i-read-hunters-of-the-edo-jidai?p=622692#post622692\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You might want to condense and repost these critiques in the thread David made, because some of the stuff, like the bits on their subgroups or the structuring of their status benefits might be useful feedback.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's cool; I'm totally going to read all of these.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>David A Hill Jr </b><br /> Freelance Writer<br /> Independent Game Designer","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/70-machineiv","name":"MachineIV","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar70_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1791"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-11T11:39:38-07:00","id":623525,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-size:18px\">Azusa Miko</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Opening</span><br /> <br /> We start with some history about the the changing status of Miko's in the early Edo period, the shoganate is imposing organisation upon religion and it seems the Miko will be made to conform. Curiously the first paragraph ends by saying that the Miko know that they will become pawns of monsters even more than pawns of mortal government. Are we to understand that monsters are pulling the strings of the shoganate? Perhaps this will be clearer when we have the rest of the Dark Era for context.<br /> <br /> Next we\u2019re told that the Azusa are only one kind of Miko and that it's possible there are other miko compacts and definitely some miko that are the enemy. At this point it doesn't say if they're the enemy because they're monster pawns/cultists or &quot;Witches&quot; - but presumably the former given the first paragraph.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">The Enemy</span><br /> <br /> More details are given about the shogunate\u2019s &quot;organisation&quot;. The temple miko now work with to spirits and gods, as typical for the WoD gods is undefined. Given my very basic knowledge of Shinto Gods are most likely just high Rank Spirits - though much like Inferno portrays demons as a template that can be applied on top of ghosts, spirits and basically most ephemeral beings I consider it's Empyrean counterpart to be the same and will treat the Shinto gods as Empyerian-Spirits.<br /> <br /> Hmmm. I just noticed that the write up hasn't actually used the word kami at all.<br /> <br /> Returning form that digression we learn that our compact, the Azusa Miko, focus on ancestor ghosts and there's no place for them in the shogunate\u2019s temples. Apparently this imbalance left the spirit/god focused Miko vulnerable to possession and manipulation by spirits/gods but also by other things. It doesn't say why. Were the ancestor ghosts protecting the miko?<br /> <br /> Fortunately this imbalance doesn't apply in the other direction. The ghost focused Azusa Miko are safe and being unaffected by the corruption they realise it exists and decide to hunt the monsters behind it. This duty is explicitly secondary to their standard miko duties.<br /> <br /> Thus far there's a fair bit of resemblance to Les Mysteries - with a blend of priestly duties and hunting. Unlike Les Mysteries they aren't a bizarre pan-shamanistic faith (that's good) or manipulated by monsters (that's very good). While both the Azusa Miko and Les Mysteries have strong community links, the Azusa have no supernatural powers which drastically changes the nature of their relationship and gives the Compact a nice unique niche. In fact I'm more excited to play a character who focuses on community links and regular miko duties like appeasing ancestors and exorcisms than one who hunts monsters on the side.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Hunters</span><br /> <br /> We have two:<br /> <br /> A con artist who's surprised to discover monsters and her rituals are real and now uses her talents for good.<br /> Another token man, who's also very good at using his bow as a weapon as well as a ritual implement.<br /> <br /> These two are well written. The con artist is a good use of wordcount by showing that yes, the popular archetype of a travelling grafter can be used with these travelling priests and fortunetellers. The token man isn't bad but adds little beyond a single character concept.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Subgroups</span><br /> <br /> Ah, these are proper subgroups. Picking one is a character defining choice, as it should be, and if you play an entire group in the same subgroup that's a game defining choice, also as it should be. Each group introduces a traditional miko role and how that applies to the vigil.<br /> <br /> Sayaniwa - social experts who mingle and keep an eye out for signs of possession.<br /> <br /> Reibai - occult experts who keep the ancestors appeased, and receive their help (usually knowledge) in return.<br /> <br /> Geki - An entire subgroup of male miko. Their talent is physical combat.<br /> <br /> Honestly, I wasn't too keen on the way the Geki are handled. I'm fine with the idea of men being in the group; but the compact is written as a female compact their sorbette is literally &quot;Bow Shaman Women&quot;, and under hunters the man is written as though he was an exception. Now that's contradicted by an subgroup of men. The Geki also fail to fit the design of the subgroups, being defined by their gender and not their traditional role.<br /> <br /> I think they this contradiction could use clearing up with men either fully integrated into the compact write up from the start or fully externalised into a closely allied compact depending on what the history makes more appropriate.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Status</span><br /> <br /> Status 1: The Medium Merit for both Ghosts and Spirits<br /> Status 3: An adaptable dot of Allies/Contacts/Resources/Retainer<br /> Status 5: Authority over rank 1/2 spirits and ghosts; and social bonuses with higher rank ghosts/spirits (does this apply to &quot;gods&quot;?)<br /> <br /> Once again we see the Status Merit providing the Endowment; though the Azusa Miko have a big advantage. They put it at the first dot and so there's no oddness about skill with the endowment only being available to high ranked members since, unlike the Ama-san, none of the other Azusa Miko's Status benefits had a thematic need to appear at the first dot.<br /> <br /> The Status 3 benefit is strongly reminiscent of the Union's Endowment: Your Friends and Neighbours. Which I'm fine with, I kind of feel that Your Friends and Neighbours (along with the Long Night's The Prayer) should be generally available Hunter Merits with the Union/Long night having a more unique Endowment to their name - and the Azusa Miko certainly qualify for Friends and Neighbours. It does however continue the pattern that the Edo hunters are getting a LOT more benefit for their Status. I'm hoping this is some sort of &quot;1.5&quot; design to bring their Status close to 2E rather than imbalance. Is it MachineIV?<br /> <br /> The Status 5 benefit, while very impressive, actually feels like a 3 dot Merit to me (or maybe six dots if you count Ghosts and Spirits separately) so that's exactly what the Mystery Cult Initiation guidelines state.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Closing Thoughts</span><br /> <br /> The Azusa Miko have a delightfully unique niche in Hunter. They combine the strong community focus of groups like The Union and parts of the Long Night &amp; Ascending Ones with genuine occult abilities; but without having enough magic or guilty secrets to distance themselves from the communities they serve. It's definitely a niche that should be filled, and perhaps a second group more suitable for the majority of WoD games (that is, modern day and anglophone nations) should be created.<br /> <br /> The sour note was that the compact contradicted itself on the presence of men. I don't mind if the menfolk are fully integrated into the compact or split off into a closely allied group but right now they seem to be both at once.<br /> <br /> In play I could easily see the Azusa Miko going one of two ways - one is to go heavily WoD where the spirits they interact with are the predators of Werewolf and, essentially, take their Azusa Miko's goals, take the standard WoD monsters, put them together and see what comes out. The second way is to try and bring more Shinto into the WoD with supernatural beings that act something like traditional Shinto folklore. Since the second one would benefit from supporting material I'm hoping something on Shinto appears elsewhere in the Dark Era.<br /> <br /> But then, I'm always hoping the Empyrean will be expanded beyond it's one sidebar in Inferno.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cThere are no rules. Only Principles and natural laws.\u201d - Promethius<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/17686-the-kings-raven-s-homebrwe-index\" target=\"_blank\">My Homebrew no longer fits in a signature, you can find an index of it here</a>.<br /> <b>Full length fan-books I contributed too: </b><a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/princessthehopeful/files/PrincessTheHopeful.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Princess: the Hopeful</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/0B9DMG5y7ZdngekVaMGNQbTF4RzA\" target=\"_blank\">Leviathan: the Tempest</a>, <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/thekingsraven/files/DreamCatchers.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Dream Catchers</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/134-the-kings-raven","name":"The Kings Raven","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar134_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3003"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-11T12:53:26-07:00","id":623593,"title":"","content":"just a little correction- there are 3 entries in the Hunters section. You forgot the warrior priestess.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-11T13:53:00-07:00","id":623645,"title":"","content":"Looking again my phone's screen was just the perfect width to make the final line of the &quot;token man&quot; hunter reach the end of the screen and make the warrior priestess look like part of him.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cThere are no rules. Only Principles and natural laws.\u201d - Promethius<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/17686-the-kings-raven-s-homebrwe-index\" target=\"_blank\">My Homebrew no longer fits in a signature, you can find an index of it here</a>.<br /> <b>Full length fan-books I contributed too: </b><a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/princessthehopeful/files/PrincessTheHopeful.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Princess: the Hopeful</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/0B9DMG5y7ZdngekVaMGNQbTF4RzA\" target=\"_blank\">Leviathan: the Tempest</a>, <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/thekingsraven/files/DreamCatchers.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Dream Catchers</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/134-the-kings-raven","name":"The Kings Raven","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar134_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3003"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-12T12:09:15-07:00","id":624540,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-size:18px\">Bijin</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Opening</span><br /> <br /> Three words in and I'm already seeing them Edo Japan's Ashwood Abbey. For the curious search tvtropes for &quot;The Beautiful Elite&quot;, the trope used to be called &quot;The Beautiful People&quot;. Lets see if my first impression is accurate.<br /> <br /> <br /> So it seems the Bijin are artists, and also servants of the merchants since the merchants have the money. As a result Edo art is designed to have mass appeal as merchants like to sell stuff to the masses. This appears to have informed the Bijin's internal culture, at least I assume it does. The final part of the opening isn't too clear.<br /> <br /> The Bijin want &quot;access&quot; which apparently involves &quot;Iki&quot; which is an innate and non-academic sophistication or a &quot;city-sexy-sophistication&quot;. I interpret that something like beautiful renaissance paintings compared to a pure white canvas. So my interpretation of this is that they want the common man to look up to them, not the exclusive elite. In short, they're ancient Japanese Hollywood stars and not whoever is making those infamous (and possibly mythical) four hour black and white films of clouds.<br /> <br /> I'm still not sure what &quot;access&quot; has to do with it. Access to the best parties? Access to the best art distribution networks. Access to the cultural Zeitgeist?<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">The Enemy</span><br /> <br /> Remember that stuff about The Vigil as a virus? I'm strongly reminded of it here. The Bijin start of as a social club and when gossiping they start to notice monsters. It doesn't say why they didn't go into WoD-Mortal-denial so presumably the art circle just had a fair number of unusually open minded people at the time. In short their founding was basically a fluke.<br /> <br /> I'm ok with this.<br /> <br /> Seriously. I'm ok with this. The WoD monsters aren't really that great at hiding beyond the individual level - my go to example is the &quot;what have you forgotten&quot; question for GMC breaking points that outright states that in the WoD just about everyone has encountered the supernatural at least once. Most people ignore the truth, a few don't, and so by the law of large numbers some groups will have atypically high numbers of people who believe. Hunter groups forming by fluke are probably fairly common, at least as a proportion of hunter groups.<br /> <br /> Finally what do they do. They kill dangerous monsters but make use of useful monsters. Oh, looks like I called it about the Ashwood Abbey.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Hunters</span><br /> <br /> Once again we have three examples. These ones I found to be a little basic for my tastes, but I'm getting ahead of myself. The examples are:<br /> <br /> A painter who realises he's painted an immortal after comparing is work to his master's. Now he paints targets the Bijin ask for.<br /> A dancing girl who killed a monster in self defence and now works as bait in exchange for the Bijin's protection<br /> The owner of a tea house who lays down the law all nearby monsters must follow.<br /> <br /> While I liked the idea that a Hunter lays down the law and monsters obey or worse (I've always imagined the Conspiracies as the biggest players in the WoD apart from gods, archmages, etc) apart from revising my estimate of their power level up significantly I don't feel like I learned anything new about the Compact from this section. Nor do I feel that the examples were things I couldn't have thought up myself.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Duties</span><br /> <br /> Like the Miko before them, these are the kind of duties I like to see. Broad character defining things. Our three are<br /> <br /> Ninki: In a word socialites. Gathering info on a monster requires access to that monster, so the Ninki lure them out with parties. I also get the impression that the Bijin just like parties.<br /> <br /> Geisha: Scholars. They learn about monsters and record what they find hidden in art work.<br /> <br /> Aware: The actual hunters, who use traps and assassinations. Curiously they are &quot;rarely called&quot; because monsters of the Edo Period police their own. That seems surprising in a hunter game, why have monsters that do the players job for them? The assassination theme also fits nicely with their cultured image (at least by modern standards, not Samurai standards). An assassin, not a SWAT team, is whom the rich and cultured call for their dirty work.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Status</span><br /> <br /> We're told that being Bijin isn't about being rich, it's about being in the right place at the right time.<br /> <br /> The first dot of status seems like it would be a lot of fun. One per session you can ask the storyteller what gossip you know about a topic and get three rumours, two mostly true and one false. The second dot refines the technique so you get to know that one of the two true rumours is true for certain.<br /> <br /> Finally the third dot is fame three.<br /> <br /> One thing that immediately jumps to mind is that this seems far more balanced compared to the previous Hunter groups. The rumour merit seems like great fun, and fits in with the GMC design of merits like Common Sense - I would actually like to see it as a merit available to all (The Union and Network Zero in particular would do well with it) that of course the Bijin get free dots in from Status.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Final Thoughts</span><br /> <br /> There's a lot to like here. They have some similarities to cannon groups; their information network of occasionally dubious accuracy harkens back (harkens forward?) to Network Zero while their primary weapon being social status reminds me of the Barrett Commission - though the Bijin have a completely different kind of social power.<br /> <br /> I would have liked to see something a bit more concrete on just how powerful and influential they are. The opening section left me with the impression that they're basically working for merchants but later on we see the tea-house hunter who seems to have the local monsters in her grip. Knowing how and why she could pull that off would be useful.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">The Inevitable Comparison to the Wilde Society</span><br /> <br /> Those who have read Dream Catchers will know about the Wilde Society. Artists who expose themselves to faerie Glamour to gain superhuman artistic talents at the cost of their sanity (side effects include insight invaluable to the Vigil, but they're in it for the artistic talent).<br /> <br /> So how do they compare; well actually apart from being artists they have very little in common. Earlier I described the Bijin as having traits of Network Zero and part Barret Commission yet being distinct from both. The Wilde Society's closest cannon comparison would be the Illuminated Brotherhood. A vast difference.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cThere are no rules. Only Principles and natural laws.\u201d - Promethius<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/17686-the-kings-raven-s-homebrwe-index\" target=\"_blank\">My Homebrew no longer fits in a signature, you can find an index of it here</a>.<br /> <b>Full length fan-books I contributed too: </b><a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/princessthehopeful/files/PrincessTheHopeful.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Princess: the Hopeful</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/0B9DMG5y7ZdngekVaMGNQbTF4RzA\" target=\"_blank\">Leviathan: the Tempest</a>, <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/thekingsraven/files/DreamCatchers.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Dream Catchers</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/134-the-kings-raven","name":"The Kings Raven","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar134_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3003"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-12T14:14:28-07:00","id":624635,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Kings Raven</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/622428-where-i-read-hunters-of-the-edo-jidai?p=623525#post623525\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">We start with some history about the the changing status of Miko's in the early Edo period, the shoganate is imposing organisation upon religion and it seems the Miko will be made to conform. Curiously the first paragraph ends by saying that the Miko know that they will become pawns of monsters even more than pawns of mortal government. Are we to understand that monsters are pulling the strings of the shoganate? Perhaps this will be clearer when we have the rest of the Dark Era for context.</div> </div> </div> </div>thinking about that, and considering the time frame, I think that it is safe to assume that Edo is a city of monsters- at least, according to the information from Beast's Tokyo, which had a parliament of monsters and a great &quot;empire of the night&quot; before the Dawn of Heroes has started.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-13T11:11:39-07:00","id":625685,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-size:18px\">Gimu</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Opening</span><br /> <br /> And now we come to Samurai, and naturally enough they're motivated by a form of Bushido that puts duty to Japan higher than honour. The Compact formed when the unification made Samurai almost redundant. No more wars, no more need for warriors. This free time let them see monsters pulling the strings. Those who chose loyalty sided with their leader, manipulation or not. Those who chose duty became the Gimu.<br /> <br /> Being Samurai they have influence and resources, which is why they arrange executions and send their retainers off to commit arson in the dead of night, as often if not more often than fighting personally. The write up says &quot;their katana&quot;, since the bow and the spear more common Samurai weapons I am really hoping there'll be a trenchcoat Easter egg because that would be hilarious.<br /> <br /> Since the compact's primary weapon is their status and influence, and since you can't hunters controlling your boss without going against your boss. Gimu caught hunting are shamed by society, and the rest of the Gimu must cut ties to avoid association. Most save up coin for post disgrace but most also become death seekers who go after monsters waiting to die in battle. Presumably it's not that much coin then.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">The Enemy</span><br /> <br /> The shortest enemy thus far at only one paragraph. They focus only on the puppetmasters behind noble houses which means they face the greatest threats, and they use the utmost discretion lest attention destroys the Compact.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Hunters</span><br /> <br /> We have<br /> <br /> A Samurai desperate to die in battle, and who fights monsters because he's too good for human opponents.<br /> <br /> A is-there-a-word-for-female-samurai? who killed the werewolf that murdered her husband and was approached by the Compact. (A werewolf as a secret puppetmaster, don't see that every day)<br /> <br /> A Buddhist who considers demons an exception to the rules of pacifism and has gnostic like views - though I understand that Buddhists came up with that idea independently.<br /> <br /> <br /> Of the three the Buddhist is definitely my favourite, as it shows potential in the Gimu I would never have come up with myself. I wonder if we'll see a Buddhist legacy in Mage any time soon?<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Duties</span><br /> <br /> The Gimu have no subfactions because being Gimu is too different from the samurai path to be taught. If I'm honest I disagree with this choice. Plenty of hunter organisations have personal philosophies rather than organised groups for their factions and given that the Gimu are going against everything they've known, using motivations as the groups would work fine.<br /> <br /> For example: Duty to Nihon, seeing the greatest opponents, and personal beliefs about monsters would make a good set of factions.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Status</span><br /> <br /> One dot: Two dots of resources for vigil use only. Makes sense, Samurai are nobility, they have money.<br /> <br /> Three dots: Four dots split between Allies, Contacts, Status, Fame. Another example of Edo hunters getting larger bonuses but straight forward.<br /> <br /> Five dots: Three loyal samurai, who once again, can teleport. Giving simple dots of Retainers would make a lot more sense here.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Stereotypes</span><br /> <br /> For the first time in Edo we have hunters with Stereotypes<br /> <br /> Reading these stereotypes the first thing that struck me is how nice they are to everyone. WoD Stereotypes are usually highly hostile to everyone. I won't go through all of them but I will mention the Ascending One's stereotype did make me go hmmm.<br /> <br /> The Ascending Ones were said to have spread across the great silk road which stats 200 AD while the Edo starts 1600. So The Ascending Ones were probably in Japan for around 1400 years. The Ascending Ones are also extremely adaptable with the modern group containing Hermetic Alchemists, all three Abrahamic Faiths and drug cartels. A group that can have Islamic extremists and Jewish extremists (Knife of Paradise section in Compacts and Conspiracies) side by side should easily have recruited native Japanese in 1400 years. So it was odd to see them refereed to as coming from the &quot;stock&quot; of a sub-Saharan African; when they originated from north Africa and probably look Japanese by now.<br /> <br /> It also struck me that he Gimu seem to know who the Ascending Ones are. Most stereotypes about them are something like &quot;my cousins friend met this weird guy with potions&quot; or &quot;My mum's aunt's grandson tried this potion and died&quot;.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Closing Thoughts</span><br /> <br /> The Gimu remind me of the Barrett Commission. Rich influential guys fighting monsters who want a piece of the rich influence pie by using their riches and influences. Being samurai the Gimu are obviously better fighters, and it seems a bit less rich and influential than the Commission, so it balances out.<br /> <br /> You can never get enough Barrett Commission stuff, so I enjoyed seeing them.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cThere are no rules. Only Principles and natural laws.\u201d - Promethius<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/17686-the-kings-raven-s-homebrwe-index\" target=\"_blank\">My Homebrew no longer fits in a signature, you can find an index of it here</a>.<br /> <b>Full length fan-books I contributed too: </b><a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/princessthehopeful/files/PrincessTheHopeful.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Princess: the Hopeful</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/0B9DMG5y7ZdngekVaMGNQbTF4RzA\" target=\"_blank\">Leviathan: the Tempest</a>, <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/thekingsraven/files/DreamCatchers.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Dream Catchers</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/134-the-kings-raven","name":"The Kings Raven","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar134_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3003"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-13T11:31:58-07:00","id":625704,"title":"","content":"I like the fit of the Samurai Compact being an outgrowth of the Edo period. During the Sengoku period, I'd find a Sumarai Compact inappropriate. They'd be too busy slaughtering peasants and each other for anything else. But the leisure that allows the cultural diversions of the Edo period is perfect for bored Samurai looking to prove their mettle through the Vigil.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-13T12:10:51-07:00","id":625723,"title":"","content":"If if anything is true in Hunter is that the Vigil is diverse. I'm sure you could fit a good Samurai Vigil in any time period where Samurai were around.<br /> <br /> Though you give me a great idea. Leisure and cultural diversions, means there's great opportunities for playing an Edo Japanease Bruce Wayne as part of the Gimu, the Batman identity would probably be something like Zuku's the Blue Spirit from Avatar: the Last Airbender.<br /> <br /> I'm surprised I didn't think of this during my first read through because I've wanted to play a full pop-culture ninja as part of the Ascending Ones for a while. If the Ascending Ones are basically the mythical \u1e24ashsh\u0101sh\u012bn then why shouldn't their Japanese branch be mythical ninjas? Part of the reason I was a little disappointed the stereotype implies that the Ascending Ones didn't have a Japanese branch.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cThere are no rules. Only Principles and natural laws.\u201d - Promethius<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/17686-the-kings-raven-s-homebrwe-index\" target=\"_blank\">My Homebrew no longer fits in a signature, you can find an index of it here</a>.<br /> <b>Full length fan-books I contributed too: </b><a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/princessthehopeful/files/PrincessTheHopeful.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Princess: the Hopeful</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/0B9DMG5y7ZdngekVaMGNQbTF4RzA\" target=\"_blank\">Leviathan: the Tempest</a>, <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/thekingsraven/files/DreamCatchers.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Dream Catchers</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/134-the-kings-raven","name":"The Kings Raven","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar134_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3003"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-14T00:54:39-07:00","id":626415,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Kings Raven</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/622428-where-i-read-hunters-of-the-edo-jidai?p=625685#post625685\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"font-size:18px\">Gimu</span><br /> <br /> The Gimu have no subfactions because being Gimu is too different from the samurai path to be taught. If I'm honest I disagree with this choice. Plenty of hunter organisations have personal philosophies rather than organised groups for their factions and given that the Gimu are going against everything they've known, using motivations as the groups would work fine.<br /> <br /> For example: Duty to Nihon, seeing the greatest opponents, and personal beliefs about monsters would make a good set of factions.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Part of this is also that they're new. They haven't really fractured, and they don't have factions, because by and large they're a loose confederation of student/teacher relationships. Give them maybe 50 years, and they'll have something we could call factions. But not yet.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>David A Hill Jr </b><br /> Freelance Writer<br /> Independent Game Designer","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/70-machineiv","name":"MachineIV","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar70_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1791"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-14T09:39:09-07:00","id":626712,"title":"","content":"While they may not have fractured yet, the impression I got was that the fault lines which might eventually become fractures already exist. You could divide the Gimu today into three common motivations for the Vigil. In time those motivations will become the creeds of acknowledged factions in the Gimu but for now they're just personal motivations.<br /> <br /> But personal motivations still make good subfactions for a Hunter org.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cThere are no rules. Only Principles and natural laws.\u201d - Promethius<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/17686-the-kings-raven-s-homebrwe-index\" target=\"_blank\">My Homebrew no longer fits in a signature, you can find an index of it here</a>.<br /> <b>Full length fan-books I contributed too: </b><a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/princessthehopeful/files/PrincessTheHopeful.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Princess: the Hopeful</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/0B9DMG5y7ZdngekVaMGNQbTF4RzA\" target=\"_blank\">Leviathan: the Tempest</a>, <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/thekingsraven/files/DreamCatchers.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Dream Catchers</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/134-the-kings-raven","name":"The Kings Raven","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar134_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3003"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-14T12:17:17-07:00","id":626784,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-size:18px\">Hototogisu<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Opening</span></span><br /> <br /> The rise of the merchant classes has been mentioned before, and now we come to the merchants themselves. The Hototogisu aren't just merchants though, they were merchants with an occult bent for about two centuries dealing with folk remedies and such. (Presumably they still have them, since those sorts of charms and herbs are common to all hunters).<br /> <br /> As the merchants grew in success they began competing with monsters, and since monsters didn't play fair they began looking for an edge against them. It's always nice to see a group who's motive for the Vigil reflects their motive in civilian life.<br /> <br /> Their Endowment comes from a guy named Inoue who discovered a way to steal supernatural powers, and like a good merchant he quickly found a way to make a LOT of money from this trick.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">The Enemy</span><br /> <br /> The Hototogisu don't see monsters as enemies but obstacle. If the expected rewards outweigh the cost of removing an obstacle they hunt.<br /> <br /> If the costs outweigh the rewards they apparently hunt even harder and try to weaken the monster and force it to work for them. Maybe any monster that well defended must have become so well defended by having skills valuable to the Conspiracy? I'm not sure.<br /> <br /> No mention is made of hunting powers because they're useful. An immortal sitting in a cave minding his own business isn't an obstacle to business, but who doesn't want immortality?<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Hunters</span><br /> <br /> The first Hunter is a merchant who guards a stretch of highway because his business depends upon it \u2013 and seems to be charitably defending other treaders.<br /> <br /> We have a merchant who just sees trading as a job, and is in the Hototogisu because she wants supernatural power. Ah, after it's absence from The Enemy the concept shows up here.<br /> <br /> Another Hunter who wants power. Or rather, hunter #2 wants power, hunter #3 is after prestige. He's also not a merchant, but a disgraced samurai who works as a merchant's bodyguard.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Factions</span><br /> <br /> Organisers are the leaders of the Hototogisu and their job is to provide sales leads and the location of monsters with desired powers to other members of the Hototogisu.<br /> <br /> Merchants are more interested in making money than the Vigil. I get the feeling that in most cases they're more likely to buy protection and supernatural abilities from the Conspiracy than actually hunt themselves; though clearly there are some exceptions like sample Hunter #1<br /> <br /> Diplomats are, well diplomats, between the Merchants and the monsters. Presumably they're also the ones most likely to actually steal somebody's power.<br /> <br /> All in all I get a very clear picture of how the Hototogisu work. The Merchants pay money and pay Diplomats to provide powers or solve problems. The Organisers are the middlemen making sure everything runs smoothly.<br /> <br /> It's nicely done, though I would quite like to see a forth faction \u201cSecurity\u201d, who're hired muscle for people who have a lot of money and no combat training Vigor is only useful if your punch connects with the other guy. Do Security get Setto? Well that depends on their employer.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Status</span><br /> <br /> Like most Conspiracies the first dot unlocks the Endowment. I've often felt that the first dot is too useful for flavouring an organisation to do nothing in of itself. Though I don't have any strong opinions about what the Hototogisu should have in a first dot.<br /> <br /> The third dot gives four dots between Contacts, Resources and Retainers. Another high dot value, I also think that the Staff merit should be on this list.<br /> <br /> The fifth dot says something extremely important about the Conspiracy that until now I've just been assuming is true. They can and do trade powers. At five dots Inoue gives you a power of five dots or less.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Stereotypes</span><br /> <br /> Compared to the Gimu these read like more typical WoD stereotypes full of snark and smugness. I approve.<br /> <br /> It seems The Agis Kai Doru are around, I thought they would be and am glad to see them. The Hototogisu arrogantly assuming they'd outlast the AKD was a nice touch.<br /> <br /> They don't want powers from the monsters the Ama-San hunt. Of course they don't, Leviathan Channels are a flat seven dots so they're immune to Setto (sorry, couldn't resist <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> )<br /> <br /> More seriously. I'm actually surprised by this. From sirens to Lovecraft sea monsters have a strong association with mind-affecting abilities and obviously sea related powers. Stealing Lovecraft powers would probably shread your sanity but what merchant wouldn't want a siren voice to lure customers into his shop or powers to make sea voyages full of cargo faster and safer? I imagine the Hototogisu hiring the Ama-San to hunt them powers; and laughing about how they can underpay peasants who don't know the true value of their catch.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Endowment</span><br /> <br /> As Endowments go this one is simple. Despite being for a Conspiracy Setto uses the single 1-5 Merit usually found in Compacts (and LostLight homebrew). Not that this is a problem, since a single merit fits perfectly. Mechanically it's very similar to Anthropophagy with the key difference that the need to con a monster results in a unique hunting style.<br /> <br /> And really, I'm padding, there's not much I can say about a single Merit, maybe if I had some criticisms but I don't. I like it just the way it is.<br /> <br /> <br /> Moving on from the Endowment itself to a broader picture. I've been thinking and discussing the question of. What is a second edition Endowment. What's Hunter's equivalent to Vampire's \u201cMonsterous out of the gate\u201d?<br /> <br /> To me the answer wasn't just \u201cmore powerful\u201d. I don't think that Hunters should match or exceed supernaturals in raw power.<br /> <br /> Actually let me rephrase that. Hunter is about diversity, and matching/exceeding monsters in raw combat power should be the purview of one or two Conspiracies. Task Force: VALKARIE in attack helicopters or the Aegis Kai Doru breaking out \u201cthe good stuff\u201d and going toe to toe with an enraged mummy.<br /> <br /> But for the rest of Hunter. I would say 2E Endowments turn monster's into prey. Perhaps the Malleus Maleificarum have a funeral rite that curses a killer with the inability to hide their sins. Performed over a vampire's victim he is cursed with bloodstains that spell out \u201cvampire\u201d that cannot be hidden. When the inquisitors corner the Vampire the Armour of St Martin is still weaker than Resiliance so they need tactics and teamwork to take down a bloodsucker, but what vampire wouldn't be scared knowing that at any time the inquisition could take his Masquerade away?<br /> <br /> <br /> So. Does Setto fit my vision for 2E Endowments? Absolutely!<br /> <br /> With Setto in play monsters must act like paranoid recluses never touching (I'd hoserule that handshakes are culturally required regardless of history) or being persuaded by a merchant, or only doing business through intermediaries. The idea that the mere presence of a Conspiracy makes monsters adjust their habits out of fear is exactly what I want Endowments to do in 2E.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cThere are no rules. Only Principles and natural laws.\u201d - Promethius<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/17686-the-kings-raven-s-homebrwe-index\" target=\"_blank\">My Homebrew no longer fits in a signature, you can find an index of it here</a>.<br /> <b>Full length fan-books I contributed too: </b><a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/princessthehopeful/files/PrincessTheHopeful.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Princess: the Hopeful</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/0B9DMG5y7ZdngekVaMGNQbTF4RzA\" target=\"_blank\">Leviathan: the Tempest</a>, <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/thekingsraven/files/DreamCatchers.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Dream Catchers</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/134-the-kings-raven","name":"The Kings Raven","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar134_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3003"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-15T02:08:55-07:00","id":627383,"title":"","content":"I forgot to do a closing thoughts for the Hototogisu so without further delay.<br /> <br /> Thus far they're my favourite group of the lot, (and having read the Otodo during an earlier teaser - that makes them my favourite). I like the fact that they have their own <i>proactive </i>goals rather than just <i>reacting</i> to the existence of monsters. I like the fact they offer a unique way to hunt, via scamming monsters. I like the fact the very existence of their Endowment will force monsters to behave differently and avoid physical contact, or avoid shopping or negotiating in person. Hattrick!<br /> <br /> Really looking back over what I wrote the only raised eyebrows were their stereotype for the Ami-San and their three dot Status bonus. When that's the only things I can bring up you know you're looking at something special <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <br /> For the curious. My second favourite is the Ama San. Something about them being, not just an organisation, but a society where everyone is a Hunter or supports Hunters really appeals to me.<br /> <br /> (you know, I really hope Hunter 2E has a capitalised word for someone who's broken free of the deny-monsters social/occult pressure but doesn't actively seek out the supernatural)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cThere are no rules. Only Principles and natural laws.\u201d - Promethius<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/17686-the-kings-raven-s-homebrwe-index\" target=\"_blank\">My Homebrew no longer fits in a signature, you can find an index of it here</a>.<br /> <b>Full length fan-books I contributed too: </b><a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/princessthehopeful/files/PrincessTheHopeful.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Princess: the Hopeful</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/0B9DMG5y7ZdngekVaMGNQbTF4RzA\" target=\"_blank\">Leviathan: the Tempest</a>, <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/thekingsraven/files/DreamCatchers.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Dream Catchers</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/134-the-kings-raven","name":"The Kings Raven","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar134_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3003"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-16T06:06:09-07:00","id":628487,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-size:18px\">Otodo</span><br /> <br /> We come to our last group the Lucif... I mean Otodo. Technically I read them already, but why let that stop me?<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Opening</span><br /> <br /> The opening section retells how the Conspiracy was formed. The most useful thing here is a description of just who the Oni are. They are not the red and blue folklore ogres, but \u201cspirits of plagues and devastation\u201d.<br /> <br /> In the WoD that could be many things. They could of course be Spirits but despite the word \u201cspirits\u201d Infernal demons are another likely option given their connection to the Lucifuge.<br /> <br /> Red and blue ogre Oni are probably some sort (actually, several sorts) of faerie. Oni are a 1E changeling Kith. But they're not going to turn up in this reading.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">The Name</span><br /> <br /> We next get an explanation of what the word Otodo means. I don't have any particular thoughts here.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">The Lucifgue Sidebar</span><br /> <br /> Two things are stated:<br /> <br /> The Otodo come from the \u201csame stock\u201d as their Lucifuge cousins.<br /> <br /> What the Otodo and Lucifuge mean to the other's dogma is up to the individual group.<br /> <br /> That said. Saying the Oni are some sort of Infernal Demon (remember that Infernal is a very broad category) would fit both groups dogma quite nicely. That's a good thing for any groups who've run the Lucifuge's dogma as being accurate or played certain Castigation powers (e.g. the summoning/binding ones) as specifically working on demons. I always ignored the sidebar about Hunters not caring what a demon is; hunters who don't care bring holy water against a Fae with the classical demon look and end up dead.<br /> <br /> Anyway, I'm digressing. Back to topic. One thing that's not brought up in the Lucifuge connection is that the Lucifuge aren't just children of demons, they're children of the Devil himself or arch-demons like Mammon. In other words, they're royalty to the point I'd expect them to have Honorary Rank. If the Oni are the same that's never mentioned.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">The Enemy</span><br /> <br /> Way back with the Ama-San I mentioned that The Enemy sections typically fall under targets or tactics. This one is all about the targets. So who's called out as Oni-prey?<br /> <br /> First off we have anything evil. The Otodo are said to be paying off their ancestors karmatic debt. Battle with evil, any evil, is the path. Even human evil seems to be on the menu as the Otodo are said to battle \u201ccorruption\u201d.<br /> <br /> The Oni themselves are targets of course, as are other Otodo-blooded who don't hunt as they do. With the Oni it's just like putting out a fire, you don't blame the fire, but with their cousins it's personal.<br /> <br /> Next up we have Changelings; and here the Otodo are in the wrong both factually and morally. The Otodo think that Changelings are descendants of something similar to Oni and treat them like their own cousins. It's said some Changelings are welcomed into the Conspiracy, it's also said some changeling\u2019s humour them, but mostly it's bloodshed. I'm surprised the humouring isn't more common, Changelings are the finesse splat after all.<br /> <br /> We also have a sidebar about their signature weapons. Interesting information in here: Changelings take Aggravated damage from iron in 2E. Or maybe that's just a Hunter thing? I hope it's in Lost 2E, with 2E power boosts a bigger weakness is appropriate.<br /> <br /> Finally Beasts are given a paragraph to themselves. The Otodo hunt Beasts, pity Heroes, but think that the best solution to just kill the Beasts first. This definitely doesn't answer any questions asked by the Beast game; but the Otodo are not Beast focused so it shouldn't. There's plenty of time for that later in the Beast Hunters book that's coming out. Honestly, I'm not sure why Beasts were given a special section here but no harm done.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Hunters</span><br /> <br /> The first hunter is basically a witcher. Raised to fight monsters from childhood, wondering the road killing monsters for coin. (The Otodo even carry a signature weapon like a Witcher's two swords)<br /> <br /> The second Hunter is actually just a backstory. It talks about the hunter's childhood but nothing after starting the Vigil or to connect said childhood to the Vigil except the obvious oni-blood.<br /> <br /> The third is basically the Lucifuge's Truth faction. Someone trying to peace together the past of the Conspiracy.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">The Lonesome Road</span><br /> <br /> The Otodo's heritage is hard to hide and given how WoD Mortals react to normal supernatural powers they're unsurprisingly hostile to the Otodo's probably demonic powers. So they travel a lot.<br /> <br /> Another witcher like trait comes in \u2013 the Otodo bloodline is thinning and to slow it's descent they have lots of sex. Geralt of Rivera ancient Japan style.<br /> <br /> This section is more important than it appears on first glance because it highlights another very important distinction from the Lucifuge. Two actually. Firstly the Lcuifuge's bloodlines typically don't ever thin, presumably the Devil's seed grows deeper roots than some random Oni's. Secondly it seems the Oni only sired mortal children once. Human-demon liaisons are adding new blood to the Lucifuge all the time. Presumably some combination of the Vigil's mortality rate, the occasional Catholic Inquisition (or similar), and the Lucifuge's own efforts are keeping demonic DNA from spreading like Gengis' Khans.<br /> <br /> Anyway back to the Otodo.<br /> <br /> In addition to having lots of sex the Otodo keep notes of their children and when two meet they trade notes. This system isn't that robust and there was some discussion about it when the Otodo were first spoilt. I never minded though \u2013 I'm ok with in character mistakes providing it isn't fundamental to the entire organisation. (Looking at you Keepers of the Source).<br /> <br /> Oooh. Now this is interesting. \u201cAn Otodo that tries to hide her heritage from her family sets herself up for heartbreak, and puts her family in danger\u201d. Take that Harry Dresden and every other superhero or urban fantasy character who keeps secrets to protect people.<br /> <br /> (Honestly it's not keeping secrets that's the problem. It's that so many of those characters are terrible at judging when secrets are appropriate and when they're not. Also, if secrets are appropriate than marriage obviously isn't. Still it's a refreshing break from Urban Fantasy clich\u00e9).<br /> <br /> So Otodo usually work alone or with other Hunters; and their non-powered family are support staff. I'm not to keen on mixed org games (I treat it as the same as crossover, which I'm not too keen on either) but Otodo + Tier 1 is ok in my book.<br /> <br /> It also seems like Otodo have lots of clauses in their Code.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Status</span><br /> <br /> All that stuff I've been saying about Lucifuge descending from royalty of hell and Otodo descending from infernal commoners? It seems like it's cannon or at least has mechanical evidence. Compare the lifespans to the Lucifuge; much much shorter.<br /> <br /> In practice that won't matter much. How many games last long enough for even a mortal's age to change significantly.<br /> <br /> Status one: Unlocks the Endowment and grants unseen sense Oni. Yay.<br /> <br /> Status three: Resources and Contacts with other Otodo Hunters. An odd choice for such a distributed and small group. What happens when a player wishes to roll contacts \u2013 they wonder the roads for a month ask ask Otodo they bump into? Maybe a network of supporters and sympathisers, un-powered cousins and people the compact helped, would provide a more logical source of Contacts and money?<br /> <br /> Status five: universal unseen senses. I actually really like the thematics of the progression here. They start of with demon blood and thus demon related abilities but as they master it they claim/subvert a demon's true place as the lord of all that is evil and thus their demon powers can be used in more general ways. (This comment makes more sense if I point out I'd personally play Oni as a form of Infernal demon)<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Stereotypes</span><br /> <br /> Not much to say here. They're nicer than the Hototogisu but not as nice as the Gimu. Apparently the Inquisition isn't trying to kill them.<br /> <br /> A Conspicuous Lack of a Faction section<br /> <br /> Once again we have no factions and I feel their lack far more than the Gimu. When I read the Lucifuge core I read the three groups as a way for players to define how they felt about being the spawn of satan:<br /> <br /> The Denial: \u201cI'm not a monster! I'm not!\u201d<br /> The Reconciliation: \u201cI like my heritage because &lt;insert reason here&gt;\u201d<br /> The Truth: \u201cI don't know what any of this means, but I want to.\u201d<br /> <br /> Later on the groups got more rigid and I never liked that. The idea that the Reconciliation's \u201cI like my heritage\u201d was tied to \u201cKill em all and let god sort them out\u201d never sat right to me.<br /> <br /> But the point for the Otodo is that I think subgroups based on what you think about your heritage, even if the Otodo don't realise they exist in character, would be a very good thing.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Endowment</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">In general</span><br /> <br /> It seems that the Otodo can replace gifts through meditation like the Lucifuge. Shame, I was hoping that would go away in 2E \u2013 as the Conspiracies are all about genetic legacies it seemed logical to me that your selection of Castigation / Seitokuken powers are the ones you're born with or with the potential for, not the ones you choose.<br /> <br /> Notably Integrity is often a good thing for Seitokuken. This reverses the way low Morality boosted Castigation \u2013 I don't have a problem thematically, Integrity is tied to the soul, so are demons in Christian theology so if that crosses over from the Lucifuge then we're golden. I do miss the low morality angle though and hope with more word-count the 2E hunter core will bring something like it back for the Lucifuge.<br /> <br /> As I read the individual powers I'll put a little mark next to each of them:<br /> 2E \u2013 this power will make monsters change their habits by it's very existance.<br /> NA \u2013 this power does not directly target a specific monster (see my comments about the Armour of St. Martin under Setto and you'll see what I mean)<br /> 1E \u2013 I think this power needs a boost to fit 2E levels.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Hannya (NA)</span><br /> <br /> This power lets you see Ephemeral Beings as though they were viable and supernatural creatures for what they are.<br /> <br /> The roll is Integrity + Seitokuken vs Dread Power + Supernatural Tolorence. That's a very powerful dicepool in favour of the hunter. Even late game you should be pushing 8-10 dice and with high Integrity you can beet theoretical maximum dicepool for a Hunter rules monster. In the context of finding prey I approve of hunters having this kind of raw power.<br /> <br /> Special mechanics like Spoofing and hopefully something similar for the 2E Mask can redress the balance for monsters who's themes deserve a better chance of staying hidden.<br /> <br /> There's also a yes/no metagame about the subject's aura. I like the idea, but I don't like the fact this unreliability makes Hannya weaker than the generic Aura Reading merit in GMC. I'm presuming the list of acceptable questions is the same; a see page XX would have made that clearer.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Jakaku (2E, with Caviats)</span><br /> <br /> This seemingly simple ability actually has a lot of surprising mechanical implications to it.<br /> <br /> To start with here's my interpretation of an ambiguous mechanical point. It says any demon or spirit the Otodo \u201ccan see or knows by name\u201d and later on it says \u201cthe two characters must be able to perceive each other\u201d. From context I think the later rule refers exclusively to humans with the Thrall condition. (I suspect a lot of people are thinking I'm being pedantic to call this ambiguous. I'm a computer programmer with Aspberger's. I am pedantic.)<br /> <br /> <br /> To start with the good. It's wraps up three Castigation rituals (Calling Forth the Pit, Shackles of Pandemonium, Mandate of Hell) into one power. About damn time; for such a core power to the Lucifuge's theme being a demon master shouldn't take three of your five power slots.<br /> <br /> It also combines awesomely with the Ephemeral Being rules. You can just say \u201cLilith come over here and eat that monster!\u201d But chances are most if not all demons and spirits will need a Condition before they can teleport over; and that means you use the Summoning rules from GMC core.<br /> <br /> Making the power combine with the GMC rules like this is more powerful to making it a ritual like the 1E Castigation rules in a situation where I think hunters should be powerful. However it also has room for the coolness of performing occult research and summoning.<br /> <br /> The mere existence of this power also means that demons and spirits will be very careful with their names \u2013 so the Otodo's demon affecting ritual is a 2E power. As it should be <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <br /> Now those caveats.<br /> <br /> The dicepool is Integrity vs Resolve + Supernatural Tolorence, which I think will mean the Otodo will be at a significant dice-pool disadvantage. If you use the Ephemerial Being rules (which you probably will as they're corebook) for rank 1-3 Spirits/demons you're looking at about 4-12 dice too resist vs 3-8 dice to attack usually.<br /> <br /> The second one is that Aspiration rule means you can't use it to banish a demon back to hell or use it to foil it's evil plot. If that rule was meant exclusively for human Thralls then it would be fine, but as written you can't banish demons with Jakaku.<br /> <br /> All in all; great power and much improved over the 1E but held back by a far too week dicepool. Fix that and Jakaku would be an excellent way to support summoner characters.<br /> <br /> I'd also quite like to see the dicepool being extra strong against Oni/demons, an explicit mention of the corebook summoning/exorcism rules and suggested situational modifiers. But the only thing I think the power actually needs is a revised dice pool.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Kaibutsu (NA)</span><br /> <br /> You turn into a red/blue ogre (so that's where the folklore came from. The Otodo not the actual Oni; I still think the faeries are involved <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> )<br /> <br /> I didn't go through with a fine toothed comb but this looks identical to Mark of the Beast but without the time limits or the after effects. Since Mark of the Beast is a huge gamble, if you can't win the entire hunt in a few turns you're at a huge disadvantage, I think that's probably an improvement.<br /> <br /> I could see characters who's response to everything is Kaibutsu, but why not. The storyteller could always throw a few curve balls like intangible foes or curses to cure rather than monsters to bash.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Kenshi (2E)</span><br /> <br /> The duration is currently listed as a week. Will it return to indefinite in the final version? I hope so.<br /> <br /> It's a neat power, very powerful, but against the Otodo's primary foe I'm ok with something this powerful. Ephemeral Entities are so dangerous that they basically need it and gifting amulets to people is a nice way to represent the Otodo's more caring side.<br /> <br /> Since the monsters don't get a roll to resist I'm assuming the power resides in the amulet's not as a curse upon the monster. This is how it should be IMO; I wouldn't let the Otodo run around making a ban like \u201churting humans\u201d on everything.<br /> <br /> I also like the way it synergies with Jakaku. You could use Kenshi to keep entities imprisoned until you want them to do something.<br /> <br /> I don't think this power would change monster's behaviour just by existing but it's powerful enough and synergies with Jakaku so I definitely think 2E.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Kigo (N/A)</span><br /> <br /> The Mark of the Lucifgue, but without the cool mysteries surrounding it.<br /> <br /> Honestly I can't think of many practical uses for this, just like the Mark. The blind fighting is too specific, and if you can inflict the mark you probably don't need to track the monster.<br /> <br /> You usually have a dud power or two in a long list so I don't mind \u2013 and maybe someone's going to point out why I'm wrong in the comments \u2013 but my suggestion would be that if the Otodo find a victim (alive or a corpse) they can perform this ritual to curse the perpetrator.<br /> <br /> Otodo then get the tracking bonus or the ability to see someone with a glowing \u201cmurderer\u201d kanji on their chest. And even if people can't see the brand they still subconsciously go \u201csomething's telling me this guy's a murderer\u201d.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Shinigami Buki (1E)</span><br /> <br /> You can't have Oni without an eating power. This one is useful, but not particularly powerful. To use it you already need to have a target restrained \u2013 which means you've already won.<br /> <br /> Sadly, it requires touching, not actually biting. I would like it to require actually locking jaws around the target, not enough to break skin but enough so the Otodo's spiritual teeth can sink into the spiritual monster.<br /> <br /> Now being able to eat a possessing entity makes for easy exorcisms and safe victims \u2013 so it is useful. The ability to create thralls you can then control with Jakaku is also a useful ability if hideously unehtical.<br /> <br /> So I'm putting this down at 1E but I'm not sure that's a problem. It's doesn't \u201cturn monsters into prey\u201d but as a utility power; I'm not sure it needs too.<br /> <br /> <br /> A 2E version of this power would be something like removing 1 random Numina from an Ephemerial Being per lethal damage as an in-grapple bite attack.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Shonetsu Jigoku (N/A)</span><br /> <br /> Hellfire attack. Got to have a Hellfire attack.<br /> <br /> It's definitely a useful power, especially in the Edo period where weapons aren't as advanced and obviously very good against Vampires and Promethians.<br /> <br /> I'd have liked the damage rating to be one higher. You're paying experience and using up a limited power slot for this. I'm not too surprised that the Aggravated damage option is gone since 2E makes Aggravated rarer, and the multiple shots per Willpower is a much needed boost.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\">Closing Thoughts</span><br /> <br /> What can I say? They're mostly the Lucifuge and I like the Lucifuge.<br /> <br /> As the first full 2E Endowment we've seen this gives me a lot of faith for the future direction. Mechanical niggles like needing three powers for a summoner character at Hellfire costing 1 Willpower per shot are gone. The Otodo are absolute terrors against their primary foes (assuming Oni are either spirits or demons) which is as they should be.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cThere are no rules. Only Principles and natural laws.\u201d - Promethius<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/17686-the-kings-raven-s-homebrwe-index\" target=\"_blank\">My Homebrew no longer fits in a signature, you can find an index of it here</a>.<br /> <b>Full length fan-books I contributed too: </b><a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/princessthehopeful/files/PrincessTheHopeful.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Princess: the Hopeful</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/0B9DMG5y7ZdngekVaMGNQbTF4RzA\" target=\"_blank\">Leviathan: the Tempest</a>, <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/thekingsraven/files/DreamCatchers.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Dream Catchers</a>","edited":{"userid":134,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/134-the-kings-raven","user":"The Kings Raven","date":". 08-16-2015, 09:43 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/134-the-kings-raven","name":"The Kings Raven","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar134_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3003"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-16T06:45:13-07:00","id":628491,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-size:18px\">Final Thoughts</span><br /> <br /> This was a good chapter, really really good.<br /> <br /> Every organisation was good. Togeather they provided a nice breadth of different groups that hit all the broad points necessary to have vigil in Edo Japan but also managed to explore new territory that Hunter hasn't covered before (the Hototogisu especially, with their con-artist ways).<br /> <br /> <br /> I've made a lot of comments as I went along but I'd say that there's only two things that, in my opinion, actually need to be fixed. That's an impressively low number.<br /> <br /> The first is Jakaku needs a more generous dice pool [edit] And the Aspiration defence needs to be limited to humans[/edit]. It's far to low to go against the high stats given to Ephemeral Beings. If the assumption was to run Spirits using Hunter rules that should be made explicit IMO \u2013 as Spirits are in the core book I think a lot of groups will run them using the core book rules.<br /> <br /> The second is more details on the Bijin; put it this way. I have no idea what resources the Compact has that allowed the third sample hunter, to lay down the law on the local monsters. I think I'd have trouble playing one without that information.<br /> <br /> <br /> Of the changes I don't think are necessary the ones I'd like to see the most are:<br /> <br /> Jakaku having a quick mention of the GMC ephemeral beings rules. Just something like \u201cWhile many demons and spirits can appear instantly when summoned they require Conditions to do so. See the GMC core pXX\u201d<br /> <br /> Not being able to change Seitokuken gifts after you choose them. It makes them feel less genetic.<br /> <br /> Seitokuken giving bonus dice to GMC Summoning/etc for Oni (maybe half dots; with Castigation giving +dots since I think I was right about the nobility/commoner split)<br /> <br /> Better Factions for the Ama-San. The current ones seemed a bit too narrow and specific. I would quite like it if hunting monsters was one sub-faction and dealing with the outside world was another. The third could be village matrons maybe. Or perhaps something completely different, but broader groups would be nice.<br /> <br /> A different stereotype for the Ascending Ones. Since the AO are based on the myths about the \u1e24ashsh\u0101sh\u012bn I'd like to see their Japanese branch be based no the myths about ninjas. The Japanese logically should be full of Japanese hunters though; they've been in the area long enough and are amazingly adaptable.<br /> <br /> Some mention of the Inferno's heavenly opposite: the Empyrean.<br /> <br /> <br /> And to end on a positive note. My favourite things:<br /> <br /> The Endowments. Dear god the Endowments, they were everything I could hope for. Scary enough that monsters would change their habits because of their very existence \u2013 thus making Hunters into hunters and monsters into prey. Seitokuken fixes mechanical niggles I've had with Castigation for a long time and Jakaku combined with GMC mortal interaction rules is just a very streamlined way to handle the demon summoner archetype in the Otodo/Lucifuge.<br /> <br /> The Ama-San. I really like the angle of a whole community where everyone's a Hunter. It feels extremely different to a normal vigil. No keeping secrets from those closest to you, more flexibility in regards balancing your commitments, and the compact's highly culture supports those differences. Plus they make good foils to Leviathans, I make no apologies for my biases.<br /> <br /> The whole con-artist angle to Setto. It's a very different angle to the usual strategies hunters use. On a related point, the Hototogisu being a group who's primary mission is completely mundane (money) is nice.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cThere are no rules. Only Principles and natural laws.\u201d - Promethius<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/17686-the-kings-raven-s-homebrwe-index\" target=\"_blank\">My Homebrew no longer fits in a signature, you can find an index of it here</a>.<br /> <b>Full length fan-books I contributed too: </b><a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/princessthehopeful/files/PrincessTheHopeful.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Princess: the Hopeful</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/0B9DMG5y7ZdngekVaMGNQbTF4RzA\" target=\"_blank\">Leviathan: the Tempest</a>, <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/thekingsraven/files/DreamCatchers.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Dream Catchers</a>","edited":{"userid":134,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/134-the-kings-raven","user":"The Kings Raven","date":". 08-16-2015, 03:40 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/134-the-kings-raven","name":"The Kings Raven","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar134_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3003"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}